{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Send by the hand of</b> — by the hand of the one you are accustomed to send, and that is Aaron. Another interpretation: by the hand of whomever else you wish to send, for since I am not destined to bring them into the land and to be their redeemer in the future, you have many emissaries. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 4:13). And Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 4:13) said: \"by the hand of one who is fitting that you send.\" That is to say: send, please, by the hand of a man eloquent of speech who will be fit and worthy for so distinguished a mission, and do not send by the hand of one heavy of mouth and heavy of tongue, who will have to be accompanied by his mouth — that is, you will have to speak through him — when he speaks to Pharaoh, for it is not a way of honor and distinction for your emissary to be a man of uncircumcised lips, since all the peoples will not be able to hear when he speaks to the king, and this will be a deficiency in their eyes.\n\nAnd the correct interpretation in my eyes: <b>Send, please, by the hand</b> of anyone <b>whom you send</b> — for there is no person in the world who is not more fitting than I for this mission. And the reason for Moses in all this refusal was his humility, greater than that of any man upon the face of the earth: he could not bring himself to assume greatness and to speak to the king, and to boast, saying, 'HaShem sent me,' nor over Israel to bring them out of Egypt and to be king over them."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:43.285273668Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}